{
  "dom_id": "step-q342697",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $y={x}^{2}+1$ for $x=x(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point where $x=1,$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2(1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
